-- ============================================================================
-- PINNED EXACT-TARGET HASH — single source of truth
-- ============================================================================
-- Included by BOTH 1.1-pre-apply.sql and 1.1-post-apply.sql via \ir so the two
-- verifiers cannot drift apart. Editing this file changes both at once; there is
-- no second copy to forget.
--
-- WHAT THIS VALUE IS
--   SHA-256, hex, over the comma-joined ordered id list of exactly the repairable
--   rows in production:
--
--     status IN ('answered','skipped') AND occurred_at IS NULL
--                                      AND answered_at IS NOT NULL
--
--   computed with the verbatim expression in 1.1-pre-apply.sql.
--
-- PROVENANCE
--   Advisor read-only verification against production project hncolwkccbbjkfithhlo,
--   2026-08-16. 42 rows. Three independent runs returned the same value and the
--   same 42-row count, so the target set was stable at pin time.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- A MISMATCH IS A STOP SIGNAL, NOT A STALE CONSTANT. DO NOT REGENERATE IT.
-- ============================================================================
--   If 1.1-pre-apply reports a hash mismatch, the repairable set has MOVED since
--   this value was pinned. That is precisely what exact-target verification is for
--   and precisely when it is doing its job.
--
--   The tempting "fix" — re-run the query, paste the new value here, proceed —
--   destroys the entire guarantee. It converts a proof about a known, audited set
--   of 42 historical rows into a tautology that always passes.
--
--   A mismatch cannot be benign. The writer regression that produced NULL
--   occurred_at was fixed in f0bc31e (2026-08-08) and these 42 rows are historical,
--   so nothing in normal operation can add to or remove from this set. If it
--   changed, something wrote to practice_session_items in a way nobody expected.
--
--   Correct response: STOP, re-run the full 1.1-pre-apply output, diff the printed
--   row list against the audited one, and find out what moved. Re-pin only after
--   that question is answered and the new set is deliberately re-audited.
-- ============================================================================
--
-- OVERRIDE (testing only)
--   psql -v expected_target_set_hash=<hash> ...
--   scripts/ci/mastery-unblock-gates.sh case (A3) uses this to run both verifiers
--   against its throwaway fixture, where the correct hash is the fixture's, not
--   production's — fixture row ids come from gen_random_uuid(), so the fixture can
--   never reproduce the pinned value and must supply its own. The guard below means
--   an explicit -v always wins and the pinned default applies only when no override
--   was supplied. Never pass -v against prod.
--
--   Because every CI probe overrides it, the pinned default is exercised by
--   nothing but production. Case (A0) of the same gate therefore asserts its
--   SHAPE — 64 lowercase hex — so a placeholder or a truncated paste cannot ship.
-- ============================================================================

\if :{?expected_target_set_hash}
  -- caller supplied an override; leave it alone
\else
  \set expected_target_set_hash '55025a91663cc7a097deb089e9a327c2ba02de79efd8654106097c4d273ce9d9'
\endif
